in Rainbow six siege small tactical  choices always lead to big consequences  every round is a new lesson in what you  could have done better with your  mistakes acting as a stern teacher  taking these lessons to heart and  adjusting your team's strategy  accordingly keeps each match feeling  fresh and exciting the thrill of seeing  your plan finally succeed is what makes  siege not only a compelling shooter but  one of the best examples of teamwork  tactics and crack shooting out there  despite its evolution over the past four  years Rainbow six siege has always been  a battle between attackers and defenders  over a single objective there are five  operators per team each with their own  special gadgets that can be used to slow  the attackers assaults or poke holes in  the defenders fortifications pushing the  objective and finding an opening to take  and protect it gives the attacking side  a steep rewarding climb to victory and  it's the defenders job to knock them  down and keep them from reaching that  summit siege incentivizes teamwork and  when a group of players execute a  coordinated assault it results in some  of the most exciting moments you can  experience in a first-person shooter  siege has a particularly large focus on  learning and utilizing each operators  unique gadget to assist in taking or  holding the objective the attacking  side's abilities range from breaching  reinforced walls and creating new doors  to dismantling defender gadgets and  using cameras to reveal enemy locations  choosing the right operator for yourself  and creating a good team of abilities  that work well together can make a  heavily fortified room much easier to  breach on the defending side operator  gadgets consist largely of tools that  slow the attackers pursuit or prevent it  altogether mute signal disruptors  disable the use of any electronic  gadgets within their range while a  well-placed welcome mat from frost can  trap an unsuspecting attacker serving  them up for a free kill shot the gadgets  are what makes sieges combat distinct  from other shooters and while you'll  likely get into at least one firefight  per round using your gadget effectively  to slow the enemy can be just as  rewarding as nailing that clutch  headshot and in siege the guns feel  dangerous some are even powerful enough  to destroy the environment each guns  strengths and handling feel appropriate  for its type and size this is  particularly true for marksman rifles  which are capable of dropping an enemy  in a single shot the sound each one  makes  fits the damage it deals on the other  end loud and lethal  there are multiple ways for both teams  to pressure each other and those who  aren't ready for it will be upended and  eliminated the attackers could be moving  toward the objective cautiously while  the defenders plan to rush them catch  them off-guard and force them to slip  out of there slower more comfortable  pace the regular shift between slow and  steady and heart-pounding immediacy is  exhilarating especially when just a few  seconds can change the entire momentum  of a match the slower moments require a  calm hand taking your time and waiting  for the right moment can be  nerve-wracking but patience and proper  execution with your teammates makes for  exceptionally satisfying gunplay and  teamwork as you take out your opponents  one by one and pull out a victory  learning the maps is as if not more  important than learning your characters  and you learn each one almost  unintentionally as you breach and  protect different rooms the subtlety of  sieges idiosyncrasies makes personal  progress over time feel profound knowing  that you can see the top of a staircase  from a certain window likely learned  from being shot from that same window  can give you the edge over a distracted  team sieges learning curve is not small  but it's not particularly steep either  you'll need to spend a long time  learning the particulars but it's an  outstanding journey with rewarding  moments that'll make you feel like  you're improving every match  [Music]  what I'm for remaining  over the past four years Ubisoft has  improved siege from something that had a  great base to start with into one of the  best multiplayer experiences around each  new season has brought new reasons to  keep playing without it ever feeling  stale the introduction of new operators  obviously brings new abilities to use or  contend with but it often changes how  previous operators are viewed and  approached as well of course siege has  had its fair share of missteps but the  game we have today has ironed out many  of them Ubisoft has proven itself  receptive and expeditious when it comes  to dealing with the game's issues some  operators have received buffs while  stronger highly picked operators have  seen the exact opposite it can be  frustrating when your favorite operator  gets nerfed  but a lot of these changes have made  overpowered characters fall in line  without completely diminishing the  satisfaction of playing them several  maps have also received updates from  slight changes to complete overhauls  nearly all of sieges maps are excellent  except for that damn favela and these  reworks have only improved their layouts  seeing your favorite operator lose a  specific attachment or secondary gadget  can be disheartening at first but their  removal grants new opportunities to  learn and grow as a player likewise  losing the hallways of a map you fell in  love with can hurt but it doesn't take  long to warm up to the changes some of  the reworked maps are now among sieges  best  ubisoft's constant battle with toxicity  has yielded some good results and while  it's not perfect it's become a much more  manageable issue that you no longer have  to feel trapped by Ubisoft has been  proactive in removing toxic players from  its game and introducing new tools that  improve the overall experience  for example friendly-fire reversal has  caused a significant decrease in team  killing as players are now able to  police themselves and prevent a toxic  player from causing any further damage  of course you can't have a multiplayer  game in 2020 without an in-game shop and  siege has a big one most headgear  uniforms skins and operators can be  purchased with the free in-game currency  there are also some items that require  the use of real money such as the annual  operator past and elite uniforms the  cosmetics are beautifully designed  sometimes changing the entire motif of a  specific operator however they're not  always handled in a way that feels fair  limited-time cosmetics and the recently  implemented battle passes can encourage  you to play or pay more than you  initially wanted or intended to these  micro transactions don't affect the  gameplay or your enjoyment of it as  siege is fulfilling enough on its own  but it's still an unneeded carrot on a  stick that comes off more obnoxious than  rewarding  Rainbow six siege has always been a game  about making tactical decisions and  dealing with their consequences but with  every new year of operators and changes  the options have been refined and  increased to make for firefights that  are as engaging as they are  unpredictable learning the various  operators and how to breach or protect a  room with them can be a slow crawl but  siege makes it easy to understand what  your mistakes are thanks in part to  seeing both sides of every match it  rewards patience persistence and  teamwork and over the past four years  sieges not only become hubie softs crown  jewel but also one of the best  first-person shooters ever made  [Music]  remaining  [Music]
 

